<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Books of Chilam Balam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chilam Balam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Books of Chilam Balam are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> written in Yucatec Maya using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet in the colonial period, named after a Maya prophet. Kept in towns such as Chumayel, Tizimin and Maní, they combine history, prophecy, calendars, medical lore and Christian material. They are a main source for the history of northern Yucatán, including the rise and fall of Mayapan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/books-of-chilam-balam.jpg" alt="Books of Chilam Balam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Reusser Monsalvez · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilam_Balam_Ixil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
